        <div class="book-publishing__body">
          <div class="book-publishing__main">
            <section
              id="product"
              class="book-publishing__section"
              aria-labelledby="product-title"
            >
              <p class="book-publishing__section-label">01 / product</p>
              <h2 id="product-title">
                From publishing workflow to working software
              </h2>
              <div class="book-publishing__prose">
                <p>
                  Editorial Denes needed an internal catalog-management workflow
                  that could keep books, authors, and collections in one
                  dependable place. Book Publishing Platform centralizes that
                  work as an in-house back-office system used in production,
                  rather than treating each repository as a separate product.
                </p>
                <p>
                  The work spans the complete delivery path: understanding the
                  requirements, modelling the publishing domain, designing the
                  API, implementing persistence and security, integrating the
                  client, testing the boundaries, and deploying the resulting
                  service to the cloud.
                </p>
              </div>

              <h3>One workflow, two permission levels</h3>
              <ul class="book-publishing__list">
                <li>
                  <strong>Administrator users</strong> can manage catalog data, including
                  books, authors, and collections.
                </li>
                <li>
                  <strong>Read-only users</strong> can consume the information without
                  modifying it.
                </li>
              </ul>
              <p>
                That distinction is a product rule and a backend responsibility:
                the client can guide the experience, but it is not the security
                boundary.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section
              id="architecture"
              class="book-publishing__section"
              aria-labelledby="architecture-title"
            >
              <p class="book-publishing__section-label">02 / architecture</p>
              <h2 id="architecture-title">
                One platform, clear system boundaries
              </h2>
              <div class="book-publishing__prose">
                <p>
                  The platform is a set of cooperating components with explicit
                  responsibilities: a versioned OpenAPI specification, a Kotlin
                  and Spring Boot backend, PostgreSQL persistence with Liquibase
                  schema evolution, and a native Android administration client.
                </p>
                <p>
                  The Android application is the current secured client for the
                  internal workflow, not the center of the architecture. The
                  contract and backend remain useful independently, which keeps
                  the platform open to future consumers.
                </p>
              </div>

              <div class="book-publishing__cards">
                {
                  architectureParts.map((part) => (
                    <article class="book-publishing__card">
                      <h3>{part.name}</h3>
                      <p>{part.description}</p>
                    </article>
                  ))
                }
              </div>
            </section>

            <section
              id="backend"
              class="book-publishing__section"
              aria-labelledby="backend-title"
            >
              <p class="book-publishing__section-label">03 / backend</p>
              <h2 id="backend-title">Keeping the domain at the center</h2>
              <div class="book-publishing__prose">
                <p>
                  Kotlin and Spring Boot provide the service foundation, while
                  Hexagonal / Ports and Adapters Architecture keeps the domain
                  independent from web frameworks, databases, and other delivery
                  mechanisms. Domain-Driven Design gives the model a language
                  that follows the publishing problem instead of the shape of
                  the transport layer.
                </p>
                <p>
                  The code is organized in vertical slices by business context,
                  with domain, application, and infrastructure responsibilities
                  separated inside those slices. PostgreSQL provides durable
                  persistence, and Liquibase makes database evolution explicit,
                  ordered, and repeatable across environments.
                </p>
                <p>
                  Spring Security handles authentication with JWTs, while
                  role-based authorization is enforced by the backend at the
                  protected use cases and endpoints. An administrator and a
                  read-only user therefore receive different capabilities even
                  if they reach the API through a client other than Android.
                </p>
              </div>

              <div class="book-publishing__decisions">
                <article class="book-publishing__card">
                  <h3>Domain first</h3>
                  <p>
                    Business rules stay testable without requiring Spring MVC or
                    a running database.
                  </p>
                </article>
                <article class="book-publishing__card">
                  <h3>Adapters at the edge</h3>
                  <p>
                    HTTP, persistence, and generated contract types connect to
                    the application without becoming the domain model.
                  </p>
                </article>
                <article class="book-publishing__card">
                  <h3>Security in the service</h3>
                  <p>
                    JWT authentication and role checks are enforced server-side,
                    not trusted to UI visibility alone.
                  </p>
                </article>
              </div>
            </section>

            <section
              id="api-first"
              class="book-publishing__section"
              aria-labelledby="api-first-title"
            >
              <p class="book-publishing__section-label">04 / api-first</p>
              <h2 id="api-first-title">
                Treating the contract as a product boundary
              </h2>
              <div class="book-publishing__prose">
                <p>
                  The OpenAPI specification lives in its own versioned
                  repository and acts as the source of truth for the API. That
                  separation makes the boundary a product artifact that can be
                  discussed, reviewed, and changed explicitly before an
                  implementation or client is updated.
                </p>
                <p>
                  Generated Kotlin interfaces and DTOs are published from the
                  contract and consumed by the backend. The implementation is
                  therefore aligned with the public shape of the service, while
                  contract changes remain visible in code review instead of
                  being hidden in an informal agreement between repositories.
                </p>
              </div>

              <ol class="book-publishing__flow">
                <li>
                  <span class="book-publishing__flow-number" aria-hidden="true"
                    >01</span
                  >
                  <div>
                    <h3>Define the boundary</h3>
                    <p>
                      Endpoints, payloads, validation, and error shapes are
                      maintained in the versioned OpenAPI specification.
                    </p>
                  </div>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <span class="book-publishing__flow-number" aria-hidden="true"
                    >02</span
                  >
                  <div>
                    <h3>Generate the shared types</h3>
                    <p>
                      Kotlin interfaces and DTOs turn the reviewed contract into
                      an implementation-facing artifact.
                    </p>
                  </div>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <span class="book-publishing__flow-number" aria-hidden="true"
                    >03</span
                  >
                  <div>
                    <h3>Implement behind the boundary</h3>
                    <p>
                      The Spring Boot backend consumes the generated artifact,
                      keeping transport changes explicit and reviewable.
                    </p>
                  </div>
                </li>
              </ol>
            </section>

            <section
              id="quality"
              class="book-publishing__section"
              aria-labelledby="quality-title"
            >
              <p class="book-publishing__section-label">05 / quality</p>
              <h2 id="quality-title">
                Confidence through boundaries, tests, and automation
              </h2>
              <div class="book-publishing__prose">
                <p>
                  Testing follows the same boundaries as the architecture. The
                  aim is not to turn the case study into a test report, but to
                  make changes dependable at the places where behavior can
                  drift: domain rules, mappings, HTTP contracts, persistence,
                  security, and the Android client.
                </p>
              </div>

              <div class="book-publishing__cards">
                {
                  qualityEvidence.map((item) => (
                    <article class="book-publishing__card">
                      <h3>{item.name}</h3>
                      <p>{item.description}</p>
                    </article>
                  ))
                }
              </div>

              <p>
                Automated validation runs in pull requests, with higher-level
                API and integration checks where the assembled behavior matters.
                This gives the platform a delivery gate that is broader than a
                local happy-path check.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section
              id="delivery"
              class="book-publishing__section"
              aria-labelledby="delivery-title"
            >
              <p class="book-publishing__section-label">06 / delivery</p>
              <h2 id="delivery-title">From repository to production</h2>
              <div class="book-publishing__prose">
                <p>
                  The production path is treated as part of the engineering
                  work. A Dockerized backend is built and delivered through
                  Google Cloud Platform, with Cloud Run executing the service
                  and Artifact Registry storing the deployable image.
                </p>
                <p>
                  Cloud Build and CI workflows automate the path from validated
                  change to deployment. PostgreSQL is hosted on Neon, and
                  Liquibase-controlled migrations keep the database evolution
                  coupled to deliberate application changes. Secrets and
                  environment-specific configuration remain outside source code.
                </p>
              </div>

              <ol class="book-publishing__delivery">
                {
                  deliverySteps.map((step, index) => (
                    <li>
                      <span
                        class="book-publishing__flow-number"
                        aria-hidden="true"
                      >
                        {String(index + 1).padStart(2, "0")}
                      </span>
                      <div>
                        <h3>{step.name}</h3>
                        <p>{step.detail}</p>
                      </div>
                    </li>
                  ))
                }
              </ol>

              <p>
                Agile planning and measurable OKRs/KPIs provided the delivery
                context around that technical path, keeping progress visible
                without making the project evaluation itself the story.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section
              id="evolution"
              class="book-publishing__section"
              aria-labelledby="evolution-title"
            >
              <p class="book-publishing__section-label">07 / evolution</p>
              <h2 id="evolution-title">
                Built for the next client, not only the first one
              </h2>
              <div class="book-publishing__prose">
                <p>
                  The Android application is the current administrative client,
                  chosen for the internal workflow and secured through the API.
                  It is not the architectural center of the platform: the
                  versioned contract and backend domain define the durable core.
                </p>
                <p>
                  That leaves room for another consumer, such as a future
                  public-facing publisher website, without redesigning the
                  domain around a new frontend. The next client would meet the
                  same explicit boundary and authorization rules rather than
                  requiring a second version of the platform.
                </p>
              </div>
            </section>

            <div class="book-publishing__closing">
              <p>
                Book Publishing Platform is one end-to-end engineering story: a
                production back office whose domain, API boundary, client, and
                delivery path are designed to evolve together.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <aside class="book-publishing__sidebar">
            <nav
              class="book-publishing__sidebar-block"
              aria-labelledby="book-publishing-navigation-title"
            >
              <h2 id="book-publishing-navigation-title">On this page</h2>
              <ol>
                {
                  sections.map((section) => (
                    <li>
                      <a href={"#" + section.id}>{section.label}</a>
                    </li>
                  ))
                }
              </ol>
            </nav>

            <section
              class="book-publishing__sidebar-block"
